{
  "title": "A token whose subformat is `authorization`",
  "source": {
    "document": "ECMA-430",
    "clause": "§5.3 Table 1 (row `token`), §6.2",
    "note": "composed: §5.3 reserves exactly two prefixes, `authentication` and `conversation`; §6.2 makes every other subformat application-defined. Ecma's reference SDK spells its credential token `authorization`, which under the standard's text is an ordinary application token (README, \"Two reserved token prefixes, and only two\")."
  },
  "wire": "json",
  "input": "{\n  \"format\": \"text\",\n  \"subformat\": \"english\",\n  \"content\": \"What is Ecma?\",\n  \"submessages\": [\n    {\"format\": \"token\", \"subformat\": \"authorization\", \"content\": \"Bearer 2f9c\"},\n    {\"format\": \"token\", \"subformat\": \"authentication_srv\", \"content\": \"a-77ce\"}\n  ]\n}",
  "expect": {
    "decode": "ok",
    "message": {
      "format": "text",
      "subformat": "english",
      "content": "What is Ecma?",
      "submessages": [
        {
          "format": "token",
          "subformat": "authorization",
          "content": "Bearer 2f9c"
        },
        {
          "format": "token",
          "subformat": "authentication_srv",
          "content": "a-77ce"
        }
      ]
    },
    "violations": [],
    "is_control": false,
    "text": "What is Ecma?",
    "tokens": [
      {
        "subformat": "authorization",
        "content": "Bearer 2f9c",
        "kind": "other"
      },
      {
        "subformat": "authentication_srv",
        "content": "a-77ce",
        "kind": "authentication"
      }
    ],
    "canonical_json": "{\"format\":\"text\",\"subformat\":\"english\",\"content\":\"What is Ecma?\",\"submessages\":[{\"format\":\"token\",\"subformat\":\"authorization\",\"content\":\"Bearer 2f9c\"},{\"format\":\"token\",\"subformat\":\"authentication_srv\",\"content\":\"a-77ce\"}]}"
  },
  "notes": "The second token is there to show the contrast: same message, one token classified `other` and one `authentication`. An implementation that treats `authorization` as a credential would disagree with this case, which is exactly what it is for."
}
